  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>790</x>
      <y>80</y>
      <w>460</w>
      <h>110</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Runs on the JavaFX UI thread. Facade calls that return a
CompletableFuture never block it; results come back through
Platform.runLater(). onStatusChanged() is called on the
indexing worker thread, so it hands updates over the same way.
latestQueryId lets it discard results from outdated searches.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>1810</x>
      <y>980</y>
      <w>500</w>
      <h>260</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>The only entry point for the GUI and the CLI.
Methods that return a CompletableFuture start their work on
backgroundExecutor and return immediately. The GUI never waits
on a future; the CLI simply calls join().
Other methods are quick and synchronous. Folder, exclusion and
privacy policy changes are rejected when indexMode is READ_ONLY,
because another process owns the index (see SM01).
modelSettings is the model for new conversations; setBackend()
changes it, and existing conversations keep their own model.
A saved conversation always opens ready to continue; when its
model can't be used, the next request falls back to instant search.
The GUI calls loadEmbeddingModel() at startup; if the model
can't be loaded, search goes on with keywords only.
Preview is a simple result record.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>5420</x>
      <y>2220</y>
      <w>390</w>
      <h>120</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Thread-safe: read by the watcher and indexing
threads, and changed from the UI thread.
builtInRules exclude credential locations
(SSH keys, browser profiles, .env files);
they always apply.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>4410</x>
      <y>2220</y>
      <w>390</w>
      <h>60</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>backendType is CLOUD or LOCAL.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>4310</x>
      <y>2620</y>
      <w>480</w>
      <h>260</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>One entry per chunk of each indexed file, stored together
with that file's attributes (path, type, size, modified time).
So filters are applied inside the query, including vector
search; a removed file disappears from results immediately;
and two copies of a file are two separate results.
remove(path) on a folder removes every file under it.
addEmbeddings() updates every file with that content.

open() takes the index's write lock if it can (mode WRITER);
otherwise it opens the index READ_ONLY ("first process wins").
Single writer: only the indexing worker thread calls put(),
addEmbeddings(), addNameEmbedding(), remove(), and commit().
Reads are safe from any thread and see the last refreshed
snapshot. A READ_ONLY process calls refresh() every few seconds.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>5440</x>
      <y>2820</y>
      <w>310</w>
      <h>60</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>extractionStatus is OK, PARTIAL,
METADATA_ONLY, NO_TEXT, or FAILED.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>5840</x>
      <y>3150</y>
      <w>310</w>
      <h>60</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>embedding stays empty until the second
indexing phase fills it in (see SD01).</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>4360</x>
      <y>5420</y>
      <w>370</w>
      <h>60</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Must be thread-safe: the indexing worker embeds
chunks while background threads embed queries.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>3660</x>
      <y>5560</y>
      <w>620</w>
      <h>100</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Adapts an embedding model that runs inside the application's process
(bge-small-en-v1.5, an ONNX model run through LangChain4j) to EmbeddingModel.
The model is inside the library's jar; nativeLibraryFolder is where the
tokenizer's native library is unpacked. The model is loaded on the first embed()
call; if it can't be loaded or can't embed a text, embed() throws EmbeddingException.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>2790</x>
      <y>2500</y>
      <w>410</w>
      <h>200</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Started only by findany gui (open(true)), in WRITER
mode. Every command that owns the index rescans the
search folders when it opens it.
Each search folder has its own library watcher and
watcher thread, and is watched recursively, including
subfolders created later.
onChange() offers a changed path to ChangeQueue if
IndexConfig includes it. onOverflow() offers the whole
search folder when the operating system reports lost
events. A rename arrives as a delete plus a create,
and the file is extracted again.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>3630</x>
      <y>2400</y>
      <w>440</w>
      <h>150</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Thread-safe. offer() is called from the watcher threads and
records the latest change time for each path, so a burst of
events for one file becomes a single entry.
takeReady() is called by the indexing worker. It returns a path
only once it has been unchanged for quietPeriod (2 s in the
GUI, zero in the other commands), or null when nothing is
ready before the timeout.
A folder in the queue means "rescan this folder".</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>2740</x>
      <y>3500</y>
      <w>500</w>
      <h>230</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Only started in WRITER mode. Its worker thread is the only
writer of SearchIndex. requestUpdate() only offers a path
to the queue, so it returns immediately and is safe from any
thread.
Phase 1, indexFile(), makes a file keyword-searchable. Every
new path is extracted, a renamed or copied file too. A file
that can't be read is retried up to three times, then marked
FAILED.
Phase 2, embedNextBatch(), runs when no path is waiting: it
embeds the chunks that have no embeddings yet, then the file
names that have none (each with its folder's name).
Status listeners are called on the worker thread.
state takes the values of the states in SM01.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>3650</x>
      <y>2590</y>
      <w>450</w>
      <h>200</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Detects the file type from the file's contents and
returns its text page by page, with its type (an
ExtractedDocument). PDF pages and PowerPoint slides are
numbered; DOCX, TXT and MD come as one unnumbered page.
Must not be able to crash or hang the application:
parsing runs isolated from the app's process, with a
time limit and a memory limit.
Throws UnsupportedFormatException for formats it can't
read, an IOException if the file is locked, and
ExtractionException if the file is corrupt or
password-protected, or parsing hit a limit or crashed.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>2800</x>
      <y>2730</y>
      <w>370</w>
      <h>100</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Adapts a document-parsing library that can run
in a separate process (for example, Apache Tika's
forked parser) to TextExtractor. The rest of the
system never sees the library's types.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>3710</x>
      <y>2820</y>
      <w>280</w>
      <h>70</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Splits each page separately, so every
chunk keeps its page number.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>3640</x>
      <y>4490</y>
      <w>420</w>
      <h>230</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Safe to call from several background threads at once:
it only reads the index. fuseRankings() merges keyword
and semantic hits by rank (reciprocal rank fusion).
A chunk is a semantic hit only at a cosine similarity of
0.62 or more.
If the CLI gets --keyword-only, semanticSearchEnabled is
false and only keyword hits are used. If the embedding
model throws EmbeddingException, the engine sets it to
false itself, logs a warning and goes on with keywords.
The ranked ChunkHits are grouped by file path into
SearchResults, so copies of a file are separate results.
Files that aren't in an active folder (paused or
unavailable, by IndexConfig) are left out.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>2780</x>
      <y>5190</y>
      <w>420</w>
      <h>100</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>run() blocks until the answer is ready, so it is only
called on a background thread (see FindAnythingService).
Holds no model client: each call is given the client to
use, so a conversation always talks to its own model.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>3660</x>
      <y>6820</y>
      <w>400</w>
      <h>170</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Bound to one model for its whole life (modelSettings,
set when it is created), so text never moves from a
local model's conversation to a cloud model.
Used by one request at a time: the GUI disables
Ask mode input while a request is running.
Tool calls and tool results are kept only for the
request in progress: dropToolMessages() removes them
when the next request starts, and ConversationStore
never saves them.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>4990</x>
      <y>5940</y>
      <w>270</w>
      <h>60</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>status is VERIFIED, UNVERIFIED,
or SOURCE_CHANGED.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>2780</x>
      <y>6920</y>
      <w>450</w>
      <h>140</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Adapts a LangChain4j ChatModel (OpenAiChatModel pointed
at OpenRouter for cloud, OllamaChatModel for local) to
LlmClient, converting LlmRequest and LlmResponse to and
from the library's message and tool types. The rest of the
system never sees library types. Clients keep no state
between calls, so they are thread-safe. Every cloud request
tells OpenRouter not to use providers that train on prompts.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>2790</x>
      <y>6820</y>
      <w>390</w>
      <h>60</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Returns one cached client per ModelSettings: the
decorated chain for CLOUD, a plain client for LOCAL.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>4350</x>
      <y>7140</y>
      <w>420</w>
      <h>140</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>A Message has a role (system, user, assistant, or tool),
its content, and the source files the content came from.
RedactingLlmClient withholds a whole message when any
of its source files is local-only. An assistant message
carries its toolCalls; a tool message carries the
toolCallId and toolName of the call it answers.
A ToolCall names a tool and its arguments.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>3670</x>
      <y>1470</y>
      <w>370</w>
      <h>80</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>One implementation per category: card numbers
and SINs (both checked with the Luhn checksum),
private keys, and API keys.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>2820</x>
      <y>1640</y>
      <w>340</w>
      <h>80</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Redactor, FolderPolicy, and PrivacyLog are
thread-safe: background request threads use
them while the UI thread changes them.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>3250</x>
      <y>1700</y>
      <w>530</w>
      <h>70</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Reads KEY=value files such as .env; environment variables override
them. CliApp passes the values to SettingsStore, which takes the
API key, the model names and Ollama's address from them.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>1800</x>
      <y>5540</y>
      <w>420</w>
      <h>90</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>One per request. Remembers the passages a search has
given the model, so a later search names the earlier
search in place of the text. A result withheld from a
cloud model (local-only folder) records nothing.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>4350</x>
      <y>7340</y>
      <w>450</w>
      <h>120</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>testConnection() returns true when the backend answered and
false when it was reached but replied with nothing. Both
methods throw LlmException when the backend can't be used;
its message says what to fix. The connection test sends its
own fixed one-line prompt, with no user data, and is not
written to the privacy log.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>Relation</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>5240</x>
      <y>3170</y>
      <w>280</w>
      <h>20</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>lt=&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;-
m1=1
m2=0..*
split into &gt;</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes>10.0;10.0;270.0;10.0</additional_attributes>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>Relation</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>5720</x>
      <y>2000</y>
